WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.400
Git.

2
00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:05.986
Once you understand how Git stores history, you can be much braver with it.

3
00:00:05.986 --> 00:00:07.700
Let's build that mental model.

4
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.000
Git's mental model fits in one line.

5
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:16.300
Commits are immutable snapshots of the whole tree, not diffs, linked into a graph.

6
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.400
And branches are just movable pointers to commits, so creating one costs nothing.

7
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:23.967
Here's a tiny history.

8
00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:26.200
Both branches grew from commit A.

9
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.467
Main points at B, and the fix branch points at C.

10
00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:33.100
Now, two philosophies for combining work.

11
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.967
Merge preserves the fork and the join, and adds a merge commit.

12
00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:41.700
Rebase replays your commits on top of the target, giving a clean linear story.

13
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.867
The cost is that it rewrites them.

14
00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:49.367
So most teams rebase their private branch to stay current, and never rebase shared history.

15
00:00:50.567 --> 00:00:52.033
And here's the safety net.

16
00:00:52.333 --> 00:00:54.900
A hard reset makes a commit seem to vanish.

17
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.736
But it's only unpointed.

18
00:00:56.736 --> 00:00:58.400
The snapshot still exists.

19
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.567
The reflog records every place HEAD has pointed.

20
00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:03.967
Reset to the one you want, and it's back.

21
00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:06.200
Let's see a drill.

22
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.567
We create a branch, commit, and rebase it onto main.

23
00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:13.034
Now a disaster: a hard reset loses the commit.

24
00:01:13.034 --> 00:01:16.133
The reflog finds it, and we reset right back.

25
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:18.833
And always look at the graph, not just the list.

26
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:21.100
Here's the story.

27
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:22.322
Friday, six p.

28
00:01:22.322 --> 00:01:22.454
m.

29
00:01:22.454 --> 00:01:29.500
You hard reset the classifier repo to test a clean baseline, and the unpushed light curve fix goes with it.

30
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.100
Old you would re-derive it over the weekend.

31
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:38.232
New you runs reflog, spots the commit, cherry picks it back, and is at dinner by six fifteen.

32
00:01:38.232 --> 00:01:39.800
Snapshots are never gone.

33
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:41.734
Let's recap.

34
00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:44.733
Commits are snapshots, and branches are pointers.

35
00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:47.967
Merge preserves history, while rebase rewrites it.

36
00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:49.933
Never rebase shared history.

37
00:01:50.233 --> 00:01:53.129
And the reflog can recover almost anything.

38
00:01:53.129 --> 00:01:56.833
Next up: GitHub, branching, pull requests and rebasing.
